{"author":{"id":"1d5f412cdeafe9987ad9876b0995bf5032718543d34c038a50cdb41808e9874c"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788305208,"nonce":"F6IuDZeDBdrandHTk6ChOhvl+7w=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDOULVc1M8AAAABPPiIPg","github-url":"https://github.com/kanya-approve/torram-ng/issues/25","origin":"github"},"title":"Recovery copies the whole base file to /tmp, then moves it across devices","message":"```python\nf = tempfile.NamedTemporaryFile(mode=\"w+b\", delete=False)\nshutil.copy(biggest_file.path, f.name)\n... overwrite pieces ...\nshutil.move(file_name, dest_filename)\n```\n\nThree separate problems:\n\n1. **The temp file lands in the system temp directory.** On most modern Linux systems `/tmp` is tmpfs, i.e. RAM. Recovering a 100 GB file tries to materialise it in memory and fails with `ENOSPC`.\n2. **`shutil.move` across filesystems is a second full copy**, so the data is written twice.\n3. **Pieces already correct in the base file are rewritten anyway.** The merge loop takes the first source with a good piece and `break`s, without checking whether the base copy already had it.\n\nBetter: allocate the destination directly in the target directory (so the final step is a rename, not a copy), write only the runs that actually need to come from another source, and use a reflink (`FICLONE`) or `copy_file_range` when an entire file can be satisfied from one source on the same filesystem — that turns a 100 GB copy into a metadata operation.","files":null}]}